<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    The interface. Start here.

    ⚠ **This file is the point of the sample.** `.vxml` for the tree, `.vcss` for the tokens and the
    utility classes for everything a class name can say are how a Vixen interface is written — the
    same three files a web project has, and nothing here is a special case for being a sample.

    ⚠ **It is an editor shell rather than a gallery, and that is doc 02's choice rather than a whim.**
    A gallery proves the controls draw; an editor shell proves they *work together* — a menu bar over
    a docking host, a virtualised tree in one panel, a property grid in another, a form of standard
    controls in a third, and toasts over the lot.

    ⚠ **A `<Menu>` nested in a `<MenuBar>`, and a `<Menu>` nested in that, are moved to the document's
    root as they are built** — every overlay is a root child, which is what lets a dropdown spill past
    the strip that dropped it instead of being clipped by it. The nesting here says what it means and
    `MenuBar.OnChildAdded` is what makes it true. The same is so of a `<DockPanel>`, which is parked
    and then placed by the arrangement rather than left where it was written.
-->
@component Shell
@namespace Vixen.Samples.HelloUi
@tag app-shell
@using Vixen.Input
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls
@using Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced
@using Vixen.Ui.Reactive
@using Vixen.Samples.HelloUi.Panels

<!--
    The window's whole layout, in one line of class names: a column that fills the window, with a
    menu bar that does not stretch (shell.vcss says so, because it is a fact about the tag) and a
    docking host that takes what is left.
-->
<shell-frame class="flex flex-col grow">
    <!--
        ⚠ **The model, handed to the whole frame instead of to each panel by name.** This one line
        replaces `Model="@Model"` on three `<DockPanel>` children, and the panels stop mentioning the
        shell at all: each one reads `Inject<ShellModel>()` and the walk finds this.

        ⚠ **First line inside the element, because document order is the rule.** The tag emits
        `shell-frame.Provide<ShellModel>(Model)` where it stands, so a component written above it is
        built before the value exists and injects null — which is why the `<MenuBar>` follows rather
        than precedes it, even though no menu asks for anything.

        ⚠ **And it reaches a panel that has been torn into its own window**, which is not obvious and
        is not guesswork: `AmbientAcrossDockingTests` walks the chain from a floated `DockPanel` and
        finds `shell-frame` still on it, because a secondary surface's root hangs off the element
        that asked for the window.

        ⚠ **What this gives up, stated rather than discovered later: a statement is not an effect.**
        `Model="@Model"` on a tag was a binding, so assigning `Model` after the shell was mounted
        reached all three panels; a `<provide>` runs once where it stands and holds whatever it read.
        Nothing reassigns it — `Program.cs` builds `new Shell { Model = model }`, an object
        initialiser that runs before the mount — and an ambient value having no reactivity of its own
        is the documented bargain. A model that had to be swapped at runtime would be provided as a
        `Signal<ShellModel>` and read through `.Value`, which is the same rule one level along.
    -->
    <provide type="ShellModel" value="@Model" />

    <MenuBar ref="@menus">
        <Menu Label="_File">
            <MenuItem Label="New" ref="@newItem" />
            <MenuItem Label="Open…" ref="@openItem" />
            <Separator />

            <Menu Label="Open Recent">
                <MenuItem Label="shader-graph.vixen" />
                <MenuItem Label="terrain.vixen" />
            </Menu>

            <Separator />

            <!--
                ⚠ **Bound to an id rather than to a click, and it is the first menu item in any
                sample that a document answers.** `document.save` and `document.revert` resolve
                through the same walk `edit.copy` above does — the shell hosts a `MaterialDocument`,
                so the route finds it — and both items grey themselves out while there is nothing to
                write. No handler here asks whether anything has changed; `IEditableDocument.IsDirty`
                is the whole of the enablement rule and nobody wrote it down twice.
            -->
            <MenuItem Label="Save" Command="document.save" ref="@saveItem" />
            <MenuItem Label="Revert" Command="document.revert" />

            <!--
                ⚠ **The first caller `DocumentClosePrompt` has ever had, and it is a document close
                rather than a quit.** `Root.RequestClose()` raises the request on the element that
                holds the document instead of on the whole `UiDocument`, so the application's own
                listener is never asked and Save / Don't Save / Cancel is about this material and
                nothing else. A shell with tabs would call it on the tab.
            -->
            <MenuItem Label="Close" on:click="@CloseDocument" />
        </Menu>

        <!--
            ⚠ **One item, one id, two meanings — and that is the sample.** `Command` binds the item
            to `edit.copy` and to nothing else: it is enabled, labelled and run by whatever the
            command route resolves that id to, which is the nearest responder above the focus. With
            a row selected in the Hierarchy that is the Hierarchy panel; anywhere else it is the
            shell's own handler below. Nothing here names a panel and no panel names this menu.

            ⚠ It is also the first `Command` binding in any sample. Everywhere else in this file a
            menu item is `on:click`, and `ShowShortcut` draws a chord that dispatches nothing —
            because until an element in this tree answered a verb, an item claiming to run one would
            have been lying about it.
        -->
        <Menu Label="_Edit">
            <MenuItem Label="Copy" Command="edit.copy" />
        </Menu>

        <Menu Label="_View">
            <!--
                ⚠ `on:click` on the item rather than one handler for the whole bar, which is what the
                C# this replaces had to do. A menu's dropdown is parented to the root so it can
                spill, so the bar is *not* an ancestor of its own items: a click bubbles from the
                item to the menu and then straight to the root, never through the bar. A handler on
                the bar sees the clicks that *open* the menus and nothing else, and the commands do
                nothing at all, silently. Written per item, that routing stops being something the
                author has to know.
            -->
            <MenuItem Label="Reset Layout" on:click="@ResetLayout" />
            <MenuItem Label="Toggle Dark" on:click="@ToggleDark" />
        </Menu>
    </MenuBar>

    <DockingHost ref="@docking" class="grow">
        <DockPanel Id="hierarchy" Title="Hierarchy">
            <Hierarchy />
        </DockPanel>

        <!--
            The gallery brings its own scroller and a panel scrolls by default, so this says which of
            the two does it rather than shipping a sample with one inside the other.
        -->
        <DockPanel Id="controls" Title="Controls" Scrolls="false">
            <Gallery />
        </DockPanel>

        <DockPanel Id="inspector" Title="Inspector">
            <Inspector />
        </DockPanel>
    </DockingHost>
</shell-frame>

<!--
    ⚠ Outside the frame, so that a toast is not clipped by the docking host and is not laid out by
    the column. `ToastHost` positions itself against the surface; being a sibling of the shell rather
    than a child of it is what makes "over the lot" true.
-->
<ToastHost ref="@toasts" />

@code {
    /// <summary>Everything the shell is looking at.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>One model for the whole shell, handed down.</b> Each panel takes the piece it needs,
    ///     so a panel can be mounted on its own in a test and this file is the only place that knows
    ///     the arrangement. It also means none of the panels reference each other.
    /// </remarks>
    readonly Signal<ShellModel> model = new(new());

    DockingHost docking = null!;
    ToastHost toasts = null!;

    MenuBar menus = null!;
    MenuItem newItem = null!;
    MenuItem openItem = null!;
    MenuItem saveItem = null!;

    MaterialDocument? document;
    IDisposable? title;
    DialogService? dialogs;
    IDisposable? closePrompt;

    /// <summary>The verbs that belong to the window rather than to anything inside it.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>A <c>CommandResponder</c> and not a second element handler.</b> The chain has two
    ///     slots past the last element — the document's and the application's — and this is the
    ///     first: what the thing this window is showing can do. Installed in <c>OnComposed</c>,
    ///     because a responder only becomes reachable when a document is told about it.
    /// </remarks>
    readonly CommandResponder shellCommands = new();

    /// <remarks>
    ///     <para>
    ///         <b>Signal-backed, because a prop that has to keep following its source is what a
    ///         signal is for.</b> An effect inside this component subscribes to what it reads, and a
    ///         plain C# property is not something to subscribe to — so a later assignment would
    ///         notify nobody and the panel would go on drawing the model it was built with.
    ///     </para>
    ///     <para>
    ///         ⚠ <b>It used to be required for a second reason that is gone.</b>
    ///         <c>BuildContext.Child&lt;T&gt;</c> constructed a component, mounted it — which runs
    ///         <c>Build</c> — and assigned its parameters <em>afterwards</em>, so every effect the
    ///         child made had already read the property once at its default and the panel drew the
    ///         empty model for ever. A tag with a parameter now emits <c>Create</c> … the
    ///         assignments … <c>Compose</c>, so the child is built with the caller's values.
    ///     </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public ShellModel Model {
        get => model.Value;
        set => model.Value = value;
    }

    /// <summary>Wires what the markup cannot say, then greets.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>What is left here is the honest remainder, and it used to be four times the size.</b>
    ///     A keyboard shortcut is a method call with two enum arguments and no attribute spelling; an
    ///     arrangement is an object; the two model callbacks are the shell's contract with its
    ///     panels. Everything that was C# because a control could not be nested — two menus, four
    ///     items, three panels and three mounts — is markup above.
    /// </remarks>
    partial void OnComposed() {
        // ⚠ **Set before anything reads them, because a panel's own `OnComposed` may report.** These
        // two are the whole of the shell's contract with its panels: a panel can say that something
        // happened and can be asked what the arrangement is, and neither half needs a reference to
        // the other. A gallery button that reached for the toast host would be two panels agreeing
        // about a third thing.
        Model.Notify = (message, variant) => toasts.Show(message, variant);
        Model.Arrangement = () => docking.Save();

        // ⚠ **The far end of the walk, and the reason the Edit menu has one item rather than two.**
        // This is the answer to `edit.copy` when nothing nearer the focus has one, so Copy always
        // does *something* and what it does is never in doubt. A panel that wants its own meaning
        // says so on its own root — see `Panels/Hierarchy.vxml` — and does not have to be known
        // about here.
        //
        // ⚠ **On the document's responder rather than on `Root`, and the difference is the point of
        // the slot.** A shell is not a view: it is the object that owns what the window is showing,
        // and registering its verb on an element meant it had to hold a piece of the element tree in
        // order to say it handled one. `UiDocument.CommandResponder` is the seam for exactly that —
        // consulted after the last element and before the application's — and until this line it had
        // no caller anywhere in the repository, which made the rule the whole chain rests on
        // unfalsifiable at that link. The order the user sees is unchanged, because `Root` was
        // already the last element the walk reached.
        //
        // ⚠ `CommandRoute.ScopeOf` is read rather than remembered: it walks up from wherever the
        // focus is and reports the nearest declared scope, so the message names the panel the user
        // was in without this handler being told, and without a scope ever being pushed or popped.
        // Both answers happen — the Inspector declares one, the Gallery declares none — which is
        // what makes reading it here worth doing rather than a line that can only say one thing.
        shellCommands.Add(
            "edit.copy",
            () => {
                var scope = CommandRoute.ScopeOf(Root.Document) ?? "outside any panel";

                if (Root.Document.Clipboard?.SetText(Model.Arrangement()) == true) {
                    toasts.Show($"Copied the layout, from {scope}", ControlVariant.Default);
                }
            },
            () => Root.Document.HasClipboard
        );

        // ⚠ **The document holds the responder and never the other way about.** A responder is a
        // table of closures and a closure reaches everything it captured, so the reference points
        // from the short-lived thing at the long-lived one; `UiDocument.Dispose` drops the slot.
        Root.Document.CommandResponder = shellCommands;

        Mnemonics();

        // ⚠ **The shell hosts a document, and that one line is what makes ⌘S mean something.**
        // `DocumentCommands.Install` registers `document.save` and `document.revert` on this element
        // for the document `HostedDocument` names, so the two File items above are answered by the
        // route and greyed by `IsDirty` — the same mechanism as `edit.copy`, one verb family over.
        // An application with two panels holding two documents installs this on each panel and the
        // focus decides which one ⌘S means; a shell with one document installs it once, here.
        document = new MaterialDocument(Model, Root.Document.Effects);
        Root.HostedDocument = document;
        DocumentCommands.Install(Root);

        // ⚠ **The dot in the title bar, which is the only thing about all of the above the user
        // sees.** Answering `document.save` and greying the menu item are invisible until something
        // says there is anything to save, and on every desktop that something is the title bar.
        //
        // ⚠ **And it is the line that could not be written until this component could name its own
        // window.** `UiWindowTitle.Bind` takes an `IUiWindow`; everything that held one held it
        // because it had *opened* one, so only `Program.cs` ever had a window and this shell has no
        // reference to it — deliberately, since a panel that reached for the application head would
        // be a panel that only works in this application. `UiDocument.WindowOf` is the direction
        // that was missing, and `null` stays the honest answer on a platform with one canvas.
        if (Root.Document.WindowOf(Root) is { } window) {
            title = UiWindowTitle.Bind(window, document, Root.Document.Effects);
        }

        // ⚠ **Save / Don't Save / Cancel, and until this line `DocumentClosePrompt` had no caller
        // anywhere in the repository** — the one modal every desktop application has, written,
        // tested, documented and never installed. It asks about the document `HostedDocument` names
        // above and hands the close back here.
        //
        // ⚠ **And it is a *document* close rather than a quit, which is the case the framework could
        // not express before.** `UiDocument.RequestClose` starts at the focus and ends at the head's
        // own listener, because the subject of that question is the application; File ▸ Close is a
        // different question about a different subject, so `UiElement.RequestClose` raises it from
        // the element that holds the document and the head is never asked. That is exactly what a
        // tab shutting while its neighbours stay open needs, and this sample is the smallest thing
        // that has the shape.
        dialogs = new DialogService(Root.Document);
        closePrompt = DocumentClosePrompt.Install(Root, dialogs, Reopen);

        // ⚠ Shown rather than bound, for New and Open. `ShowShortcut` draws the chord on the right of
        // the row and registers nothing — there is no keymap in this sample, so an item claiming ⌘N
        // opened something would be lying about it. Save is the one that is now bound, and its chord
        // is still only drawn: a menu item is reachable by clicking it whatever the keyboard does.
        newItem.ShowShortcut(InputKey.N, ModifierKeys.Control);
        openItem.ShowShortcut(InputKey.O, ModifierKeys.Control);
        saveItem.ShowShortcut(InputKey.S, ModifierKeys.Control);

        // ⚠ **After the panels, not before.** A panel the arrangement does not already mention goes
        // into the first group, so three panels registered against an empty layout give one group of
        // three tabs — correct, and not an editor. Applying it afterwards is also the order an
        // application uses when it has no saved layout to restore.
        docking.SetLayout(ShellModel.DefaultLayout());

        toasts.Show("Welcome to Vixen", ControlVariant.Primary, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(6));

        // ⚠ The document's tick, not the recogniser's. It raises `UiDocument.Ticked` — which is what
        // an `Overlay`'s delay and a `Toasts` dismissal hang on — and advances the CSS animator,
        // without which the `transition` shell.vcss declares on a card sticks at the value it was
        // leaving rather than moving to the new one. Subscribing here rather than asking the host to
        // call us is what keeps `Program.cs` a bootstrap: a toast that expires is the toast host's
        // business and not the frame loop's.
        Root.Document.Ticked += Expire;
    }

    /// <inheritdoc />
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>The one thing the build region cannot take care of, and it matters here because
    ///     <c>OnComposed</c> runs more than once.</b> Effects, bindings and the elements themselves
    ///     belong to the region and go with it; a handler on the <i>document</i> outlives this
    ///     component. A markup hot reload unmounts and rebuilds on the same object — so without this,
    ///     every save would leave another subscription behind, each one ticking the <c>ToastHost</c>
    ///     of a build that no longer exists.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnUnmounted() {
        Root.Document.Ticked -= Expire;

        // The document watches the model with an effect of its own, made against the document's
        // scheduler rather than this build region — so it is this component's to stop, for the same
        // reason and on the same line as the tick above.
        document?.Dispose();
        document = null;

        // The title effect is made against the document's scheduler for the same reason, so a hot
        // reload that rebuilt this component would otherwise leave one behind writing the window's
        // title from a `MaterialDocument` nothing else holds.
        title?.Dispose();
        title = null;

        // The prompt is a handler on `Root`, which is this component's element and goes with the
        // build region — but the handle is what `RemoveHandler` needs to be given the same delegate
        // instance, and the service owns an overlay on the document. Both are this component's.
        closePrompt?.Dispose();
        closePrompt = null;

        dialogs?.Dispose();
        dialogs = null;

        base.OnUnmounted();
    }

    /// <summary>Shuts the document the shell is holding and opens an empty one.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>What <c>DocumentClosePrompt</c> calls once the answer allows it, and it is not a
    ///     quit.</b> The prompt takes the close action from its caller precisely so that the same
    ///     modal can serve a window closing, an application quitting and — this — one document being
    ///     put down while the window stays. A sample with a second document would pass its own
    ///     removal instead, and nothing about the prompt would change.
    ///     <para>
    ///         The old document is disposed because it holds an effect watching the model; the new
    ///         one takes the model as it now stands, so <c>IsDirty</c> starts false against a fresh
    ///         snapshot rather than against the one the closed document had saved.
    ///     </para>
    /// </remarks>
    void Reopen() {
        document?.Dispose();

        document = new MaterialDocument(Model, Root.Document.Effects);
        Root.HostedDocument = document;

        // ⚠ Rebound rather than left alone. The title's effect reads the *document's* signals, so a
        // shell that swapped the document and kept the binding would go on writing the name and the
        // dirty dot of the one it had just disposed — a window titled after a document that is no
        // longer open, which is the kind of wrong nothing throws about.
        title?.Dispose();
        title = Root.Document.WindowOf(Root) is { } window
            ? UiWindowTitle.Bind(window, document, Root.Document.Effects)
            : null;

        toasts.Show("Closed the material");
    }

    /// <summary>Asks the document whether it may go, from the element that holds it.</summary>
    void CloseDocument() => Root.RequestClose();

    /// <summary>Lets the toasts count down.</summary>
    void Expire(UiDocument document, TimeSpan now) => toasts.Tick(now);

    /// <summary>Turns the <c>_</c> in each menu's name into an access key, and takes the marker out.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     <para>
    ///         ⚠ <b>Explicit, because <c>AccessKey.Parse</c> is a convention and not a behaviour.</b>
    ///         Nothing in the framework reads a marker out of a label on its own, deliberately: a
    ///         control that did would claim <c>c</c> as the access key of a button labelled
    ///         <c>snake_case</c>, and the collision would only show up when somebody held Alt. So the
    ///         rule is written down once in <c>Vixen.Ui</c> and applied by whoever wants it — which
    ///         until this method was nobody, leaving <c>UiElement.AccessKey</c> a property that
    ///         <c>ButtonBase</c> knew how to answer and nothing ever set.
    ///     </para>
    ///     <para>
    ///         The bar's items rather than the menus: a <c>MenuBarItem</c> is the <c>ButtonBase</c>
    ///         that has the name on it and answers an <c>AccessKeyEvent</c> by opening its menu, and
    ///         a <c>Menu</c> is an overlay that never draws the word at all.
    ///     </para>
    /// </remarks>
    void Mnemonics() {
        foreach (var item in menus.Items) {
            item.Label = AccessKey.Parse(item.Label, out var key);
            item.AccessKey = key;
        }
    }

    void ResetLayout() {
        docking.SetLayout(ShellModel.DefaultLayout());
        toasts.Show("Layout reset");
    }

    /// <summary>Swaps every colour in the control set, with one class on the root.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     <c>ControlTheme.vcss</c> declares its palette twice — <c>root</c> and <c>root.dark</c> —
    ///     as custom properties, so this changes nothing but which of the two blocks wins, and every
    ///     control follows because every control reads the tokens.
    /// </remarks>
    void ToggleDark() {
        if (!Root.Document.Root.RemoveClass("dark")) {
            Root.Document.Root.AddClass("dark");
        }
    }
}
